title: Overview - Phase Timeline (v1.0)

''Reference timeline of state changes during a parse.'' For the step-by-step narrative see [[Overview - Parse Pipeline (v1.0)]].

A Gantt-style view: the four TW parser phases run left-to-right across the top, and ten tracks show ''what happens to each piece of state'' along the way.

[img[cm-v1-phase-timeline.svg]]

!! How to read the chart

* The top bar shows the four parser phases (A) `instantiateRules`, (B) `parsePragmas` (wrapped), (C) `parseBlocks` / `parseInlineRun`, (D) Render. Phase widths are illustrative, not real-time.
* Each row is a ''track'' — one piece of state or one activity. Solid bars are active mutation; dashed bars mean "in effect but read-only".
* Diamond markers (◇) are ''instantaneous events'' — flag flips, single function calls.

!! Cross-track glue worth flagging

Most rows are self-explanatory from the bar geometry. These are the non-obvious dependencies between tracks:

; Row 4 (registry.disabledCoreRules) → Row 5 (TW core wikirules)
: The set is accumulated in phase A but ''applied'' at the start of phase B (the red diamond on row 5). That's why row 4's bar is solid in A then dashed in B; the *removal* itself happens on row 5.

; Row 6 (parser flags) → Row 8 (findNextMatch)
: The green `_cmPragmasDone = true` diamond on row 6 is what flips row 8's behaviour from "accept anything" to "filter by active set". Without that gate, the active-set filter would also reject vocabs that `\importcustom` only activated moments earlier in phase B.

; Row 7 (User pragmas) → Row 3 (registry.active)
: `\importcustom` is the only user pragma that mutates registry state — it pushes new opens into row 3's `active` set, which is why row 3's bar continues into phase B before freezing.

!! Why this exact ordering?

See [[Overview - Parse Pipeline (v1.0)]] § Why the multi-stage dance.

